W7 (#11): hierarchical sky130hd place & route of sigil_soc_top for the Caravel user area
Branch 11-sky130-hier; host vivado_rnd (r7i.4xlarge, 16 vCPU), ORFS image
openroad/orfs@sha256:63981d1ac8990a9bee8bf874c95ee2c1ee3fe7b99f57d459282ab8c31a8ed663,
<= 12 threads in total; 2026-10-22 (UTC). Config: flow/designs/sky130hd/sigil_hier/
(top config.mk + sigil_cpu/ + sigil_crypto_top/ block configs). Same RTL and
sky130 configuration as the flat run (w7-sky130-pnr-2026-10-21.log):
SIGIL_SRAM_SKY130, 4 KiB direct-mapped L1s, 7 OpenRAM macros. RTL unchanged.

RESULT: DOES NOT FIT THE CARAVEL USER AREA; NO GDS. The hierarchical
flow works (blocks harden to LEF/LIB abstracts with met4 power pins,
the top synthesizes against block stubs, connects block power with
met5, places, runs CTS and detail-routes; top-level DRT converged to
1,009 violations by iteration 15 on provisional abstracts). The
crypto block detail-routes in its Caravel slot (converging, see
below). The CPU block does not: in its 7.42 mm^2 share of the
2920 x 3520 um die, global routing overflows on 45k-101k gcells at
77-78 % usage; it routes cleanly in global routing only at ~11.2 mm^2
(9.9 mm^2 still overflows). Whole SoC on sky130hd: ~14 mm^2, ~1.4x
the Caravel user area.
Owner decision 2026-10-22: build the whole SoC (RTL unchanged) on a
larger non-Caravel die; plan and runs in the last section ("Non-Caravel
die").

== Partition
  sigil_cpu         core + vector unit + FPU + L1 I$/D$ + bus arbiter + the
                    7 OpenRAM macros (macros stay with their logic: the
                    VRF and cache arrays have hundreds of pins each)
  sigil_crypto_top  SHA-256/512, Keccak, EC field unit, AXI4-Lite slave
                    (95 pins)
  top               reset synchronizer, fabric, CLINT, PLIC, UART, boot
                    ROM, crypto MMIO bridge (0.060 mm^2 of std cells)
  Block convention (Caravel-style macro): signals on met1-met4 only,
  power = met1 rails + met4 straps exported as VDD/VSS pins
  (block_pdn.tcl); the CPU block also exports the met4 straps over the
  OpenRAM vdd/gnd pin columns (sigil_cpu/post_pdn.tcl, 42 straps). The
  top's met5 stripes cross the blocks and via4 onto those pins
  (pdn.tcl). Abstract check: write_abstract_lef emits the met4 straps
  as PIN VDD/VSS and OBS li1-met4 only (met5 free over the blocks).

== Synthesis (per block, ORFS flat synthesis of each block)
  sigil_crypto_top  1,074,322 um^2 std cells (sequential 41.6 %), 100,776
                    cells; wall 249 s
  sigil_cpu         4,809,886 um^2 = 1,895,866 std cells + 2,914,020 macros,
                    209,935 cells; wall 2,904 s
  top glue          59,617 um^2; wall 22 s
  Note the split: the crypto engine is 36 % of the SoC's 2.97 mm^2 of std
  cells (Keccak f1600 + SHA-512 since W3).

== Top-level synthesis with hard blocks (why ORFS BLOCKS is not used)
  ORFS BLOCKS puts the block .libs into ADDITIONAL_LIBS for synthesis and
  blackboxes the block modules from their liberty view. yosys-slang gives
  the blackboxed u_cpu cell all of sigil_cpu's parameters, so
  `hierarchy -check` fails: "Module `sigil_cpu' ... does not have a
  parameter named 'C1_SIZE'" (same with SYNTH_BLACKBOXES). Working
  setup: synthesize the top from port-only stubs of the blocks
  (stubs/gen_stubs.sh, from the RTL headers) kept as blackboxes by
  slang --empty-blackboxes, without the block libs (SIGIL_TOP_SYNTH=1
  in the environment of the synth run), and strip the block-instance
  parameters in SYNTH_CANONICALIZE_TCL (stubs/strip_params.tcl):
  OpenSTA cannot parse "sigil_cpu #(...) u_cpu". Later stages read the
  block LEF/LIB/GDS through ADDITIONAL_LEFS/LIBS/GDS.

== Caravel budget (die 2920 x 3520 um = 10.28 mm^2)
  v1 floorplan: CPU 2900 x 2560 (7.42 mm^2) at the bottom, crypto
  2590 x 850 (2.20 mm^2) at the top, glue in a 90 um channel between
  them. v2 (current): 130 um glue column on the left die edge, CPU
  2770 x 2680 (7.42 mm^2) with pins on its left edge, crypto
  2770 x 780 (2.16 mm^2) above it with pins on its bottom edge (reached
  through a 40 um gap), SoC pins on the left die edge.
  Std-cell density this leaves: crypto 49 % of its core (55 % after
  placement repair/CTS); CPU 1.90 mm^2 in the 7.42 mm^2 block of which
  3.70 mm^2 is macros + 40 um halos (~51 %).

== Detailed-routing calibration (stock ORFS sky130hd/aes, same image, 1 thread)
  18,448 instances, 55 % utilization: DRT violations per iteration
  19,752 / 15,827 / 14,498 / 3,795 / 753 / 126 / 62 / 0 (iteration 7),
  route rc=0, wall 4,909 s. Iterations 0-2 rip up and re-route
  everything; the drop starts at iteration 3 (marker-driven rip-up).
  So iteration 0-2 counts alone do not show non-convergence: the
  flat run (1,036,832 / 842,148 / 833,572 at 30 % of iteration 2) was
  stopped before the first iteration that normally shows convergence,
  and its 0.80 iteration-1 ratio is the same as aes's.

